/// Ownership: the marker that makes a type responsible for something it must release.
///
/// A type implementing `Drop` becomes move-only, and so does any aggregate holding one, because a value with a
/// destructor cannot be duplicated by copying its bytes. Passing, returning, initializing or assigning one consumes
/// it, and reading it afterwards is rejected with a use-after-move diagnostic. That much is enforced today, and it is
/// what makes `Drop` worth implementing now: it is how a type says that its value has one owner at a time.
///
/// Implement it with `extend MyType : Drop`, which is what marks the type droppable; declaring a method named `Drop`
/// without the interface does not.
///
/// The compiler runs it once per value that was not moved away, before the type's fields, elements, and enum payloads
/// are released in reverse construction order. That happens at ordinary scope exit, at a return, at `break` and
/// `continue`, on the value an assignment overwrites, and where `?` leaves the function early. A value that was handed
/// to someone else is not destroyed where it used to live, on every path — including one that moved it away only under
/// a condition.
///
/// Panic and process termination do not unwind, so `Drop` does not run on those paths. A resource whose release can
/// fail should therefore also offer an explicit `Close` that consumes the value and reports the error; `Drop` has no
/// way to report one.
///
/// # Safety
///
/// Two things are still outstanding. Destruction of the parts already built when constructing an aggregate fails is
/// planned but not emitted, so a constructor that abandons a half-built value leaks the fields it had finished. And an
/// enum whose payload lives packed beside its tag — a non-generic enum carrying a value — has no well-defined home for
/// a droppable payload, so that payload is left alone rather than destroyed through a truncated copy.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/drop
interface Drop {
    /// Releases whatever this value owns.
    ///
    /// Borrows the value through a `*var` receiver rather than consuming it: the storage is still there and the
    /// compiler reclaims it once this returns.
    ///
    /// Must not fail and must not panic, because there is no caller to report to and no way to stop the destruction
    /// already under way.
    ///
    /// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/drop/drop
    func Drop();
}
